<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <!--
      Enabled so the SharpDX versions below actually take effect. They arrive only as MonoGame's
      transitive dependencies, and without transitive pinning a PackageVersion for a package no
      project references directly is silently ignored.
    -->
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>

    <!--
      3.8.5.1 is the current release and the one to be on. It is not a cosmetic bump: its
      mgfxc fixed a bug in MonoGame's MojoShader fork that rejected every `if (boolUniform)`
      in a Shader Model 3 shader ("IF src0 must have replicate swizzle" - see PORTING-NOTES.md).
      3.8.2, 3.8.3 and 3.8.4.1 all carry that bug, which made SM3 look unusable for the
      DesktopGL target when it is not.

      MGFX container versions across releases, read from Effect.ReadHeader:
        3.8.2.1105        MGFXVersion 10, no MGFXMinVersion  -> accepts exactly 10
        3.8.3 / 3.8.4.1   MGFXVersion 10, no MGFXMinVersion  -> accepts exactly 10
        3.8.5.x           MGFXVersion 11, MGFXMinVersion 10  -> accepts 10 AND 11

      So tools/MgfxTranscode deliberately still targets v10: it is the only version accepted
      by every 3.8.2+ runtime, which keeps the transcoded effects portable across all of them.
      mgfxc 3.8.5.1 EMITS v11, which a 3.8.2 runtime would reject - so the tool version in
      .config/dotnet-tools.json and this runtime pin must move together.
    -->
    <MonoGameVersion>3.8.5.1</MonoGameVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="MonoGame.Framework.WindowsDX" Version="$(MonoGameVersion)" />
    <PackageVersion Include="MonoGame.Framework.DesktopGL" Version="$(MonoGameVersion)" />
    <PackageVersion Include="MonoGame.Content.Builder.Task" Version="$(MonoGameVersion)" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Steamworks.NET" Version="2024.8.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Mono.Cecil" Version="0.11.6" />

    <!--
      HarmonyLib - the runtime patching framework the mod loader is built on.

      This version is part of the port's public API in a way most packages are not: mods compile
      against 0Harmony.dll and the game ships exactly one copy of it, because two Harmony
      instances patching the same methods from different assemblies is a debugging nightmare. So
      raising this can break already-built third-party mods, and it should move deliberately and
      be noted in MODDING.md when it does.

      Unlike the SharpDX pin above, this one is safe to verify: the game references Harmony
      directly, so an API break is a compile error rather than a startup TypeLoadException. It is
      still checked at runtime - tools/DataExport loads a real mod through the loader and asserts
      the patch took effect, which is what proves Harmony works on .NET 8 at all.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Lib.Harmony" Version="2.4.2" />

    <!--
      SharpDX, pinned to EXACTLY what MonoGame asks for. Do not raise it.

      MonoGame.Framework.WindowsDX 3.8.5.1 depends on SharpDX 4.0.1 across seven packages. The
      game itself references none of them - the dependency is entirely inside MonoGame's
      WindowsDX backend.

      4.2.0 is the last release SharpDX made, and pinning forward to it looked free. It is not:
      SharpDX changed an interface that MonoGame's own `Callback` type implements, so the
      already-compiled MonoGame.Framework no longer satisfies it and the game dies at startup
      with

          The type initializer for 'Microsoft.Xna.Framework.Media.MediaPlayer' threw an exception
          Method 'QueryInterface' in type 'Callback' from assembly 'MonoGame.Framework' does not
          have an implementation

      from MediaPlayer's static constructor, by way of AudioManager's. Raising a transitive
      dependency that a PRECOMPILED assembly implements interfaces from is only safe if that
      assembly is rebuilt against it, which would mean forking MonoGame.

      Nothing caught this at build time - the game does not reference SharpDX, so there is no
      compile-time check - and the asset probe passed clean, because loading content never
      touches MediaFoundation. It took launching the game. ContentProbe now reads
      MediaPlayer.State up front for exactly this reason (ProbeMediaSubsystem), so the failure
      is a nonzero exit code rather than a crash report from a modder; that check is confirmed
      to fail on 4.2.0 and pass on 4.0.1.

      These pins are therefore a guard, not an upgrade: they hold the version MonoGame was
      built against even if some other package tries to pull a newer one in.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="SharpDX" Version="4.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SharpDX.DXGI" Version="4.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SharpDX.Direct2D1" Version="4.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SharpDX.Direct3D11" Version="4.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SharpDX.MediaFoundation" Version="4.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SharpDX.XAudio2" Version="4.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SharpDX.XInput" Version="4.0.1" />

    <!--
      TOOL-ONLY as of PORT DEVIATION 12. The game no longer references this at all.

      CustomXmlSerializer used to generate its XML-proxy types at runtime with CodeDom, and
      referencing this package was what let that code compile on both targets - on .NET
      Framework the types were in the BCL, on .NET 8 they are type-forwarded here. The proxies
      are now generated at build time by tools/XmlProxyGen, which owns the whole CodeDom half
      (tools/XmlProxyGen/ProxyCodeGenerator.cs is the game's former generator, moved).

      Worth keeping straight: the package provides code GENERATION only. CompileAssemblyFromDom
      throws PlatformNotSupportedException on .NET Core and later, which is exactly why the
      runtime path had to go. Moving rather than merely bypassing it matters most to DesktopGL:
      that target builds as net8.0, where this is a real package, whereas WindowsDX's
      net8.0-windows gets System.CodeDom free from the Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App framework.
      Both publish outputs are now free of it - see PORTING-NOTES.md deviation 12.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="System.CodeDom" Version="8.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
